Package: Elections.BBMM
Type: Package
Title: Voting Blocs from Referendum Returns with Beta-Binomial Mixtures
Version: 1.0.0
Authors@R: c(
    person("John D.", "O'Brien", email = "jobrien@bowdoin.edu", role = c("aut", "cre", "cph"),
           comment = c(ORCID = "0000-0001-8958-7749")),
    person("Nathan", "Kellerman", role = "ctb",
           comment = paste("core EM algorithm, contributed as his undergraduate honors thesis",
                           "'A Rapid Algorithm for Inferring Latent Mixture Structure in",
                           "Replicate Social Science Data' (Bowdoin College, 2026)")),
    person("Aaron", "Kaufman", role = "ctb",
           comment = "New York University Abu Dhabi; developed many of the tools for exploring model results"))
Description: Identifies voting blocs - groups of places that vote alike
    across many referendums - by fitting the beta-binomial mixture model of
    O'Brien (2026) <doi:10.1017/psrm.2025.10050> to a panel of two-option
    election returns (units such as precincts or towns by questions). Provides a tempered expectation-maximisation fit with a fixed
    dispersion and model-selection rules, a fixed-K Markov chain Monte Carlo
    sampler for the coarsened posterior of Miller and Dunson (2019)
    <doi:10.1080/01621459.2018.1469995> with split-merge moves after Jain
    and Neal (2004) <doi:10.1198/1061860043001>, and stepping-stone
    sequential Monte Carlo (Del Moral, Doucet and Jasra 2006
    <doi:10.1111/j.1467-9868.2006.00553.x>) for the marginal likelihood of
    each number of blocs. Posterior summaries, maps, bloc profiles and
    Jensen-Shannon principal-coordinate plots work on the output of all
    three. Includes Maine's statewide referendums of 2008-2025 by town.
